===================================================
# DNS SEEDER for P2P Crypto-Currency (ECOin) #
===================================================

The seeder is a crawler for the ECOin network that exposes a list of reliable
nodes through a built-in DNS server. A wallet starting with no known peers asks
this DNS seed for addresses, so it can join the network without anyone handing
it an IP by hand.

Features:

    - regularly revisits known nodes to check their availability
    - bans nodes after enough failures, or bad behaviour
    - only serves nodes on the P2P port, with NODE_NETWORK, a protocol version
      of at least 70007 and a block height above the floor set in db.h
    - keeps statistics over windows of 2 hours, 8 hours, 1 day, 1 week and
      1 month, to base decisions on
    - very low memory (a few tens of megabytes) and cpu requirements
    - crawlers run in parallel (96 threads by default)

===================================================

(0.) Requirements:

    - g++, OpenSSL (-lcrypto) and the Boost headers

    sudo apt-get install build-essential libssl-dev libboost-all-dev

(1.) Build and run:

    See HOWTO.

===================================================

+ How the DNS seed is reached:

The seed hostname must be delegated to the machine running the seeder, which
answers DNS queries for it directly. For ecoinseed.03c8.net the NS record points
to the server itself:

    $ dig -t NS ecoinseed.03c8.net

    ;; ANSWER SECTION
    ecoinseed.03c8.net.   86400    IN   NS   ecoinseed.03c8.net.

A query then travels like this:

    1. the wallet asks its own DNS server for ecoinseed.03c8.net
    2. that server walks up the chain looking for who is authoritative
    3. the authoritative server for 03c8.net answers that the seed host itself
       is the nameserver for that name
    4. the query reaches this machine on port 53
    5. the seeder answers with a set of node addresses

Note that ECOin wallets do not rely on the resolver alone: the client sends its
query straight to port 53 of the host the seed name resolves to, and only falls
back to a plain lookup if that fails. So the seeder has to be reachable on port
53 from the internet, UDP and TCP.

DNS changes may take a few hours to propagate. Provide an e-mail address with -m
if you want the DNS server to report SOA records.

===================================================

+ Running as non-root:

Binding port 53 normally needs root. Two ways around it:

    sudo setcap cap_net_bind_service=+eip /path/to/dnsseed

or a redirect to an unprivileged port, running the seeder with -p 5353:

    sudo iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -p udp --dport 53 -j REDIRECT --to-port 5353

===================================================

+ ECOin network parameters:

These have to match the wallet or no node will ever answer the crawler:

    magic (mainnet)    cf d1 e8 ea      protocol.cpp   <- src/main.cpp
    magic (testnet)    cd f2 c0 ef      main.cpp       <- src/main.cpp
    P2P port           7408 / 17408     protocol.h     <- src/protocol.h
    protocol version   70009            serialize.h    <- src/version.h
    minimum version    70007            db.h           <- src/version.h
    bootstrap nodes    mainnet_seeds    main.cpp
    minimum height     GetRequireHeight db.h

Two traps when adapting this crawler from another coin:

    - ECOin serializes network addresses inside the version message WITH their
      4-byte timestamp, because the initial stream version is 70007 and
      CADDR_TIME_VERSION is 70000. A crawler that sends the shorter Bitcoin-style
      addresses is silently ignored by every node, and never validates a single
      one of them.

    - GetRequireHeight() rejects nodes below a fixed block height. A value
      inherited from another chain rejects the whole network. Raise it as the
      chain grows, the same way checkpoints are updated.

===================================================
